<section
  class="breath-education"
  id="cosmic-breath-field-guide"
  aria-labelledby="breath-education-title"
>
  <header class="breath-education__intro">
    <p class="eyebrow">Cosmic Breath field guide</p>
    <h2 id="breath-education-title">Explore the framework</h2>
    <p>
      Read the explorer as an indexed structural sequence within Cosmic Universalism.
      This guide explains its names, notation, boundaries, and interpretive limits.
    </p>

  </header>

  <section
    class="education-card education-card--wide"
    id="cosmic-breath-at-a-glance"
    aria-labelledby="cosmic-breath-at-a-glance-title"
  >
    <div class="section-heading">
      <span aria-hidden="true">01</span>
      <div>
        <h3 id="cosmic-breath-at-a-glance-title">Cosmic Breath at a Glance</h3>
        <p>
          Cosmic Universalism represents one Cosmic Breath through 51 selectable
          structural TOM states: 26 expansion states followed by 25 compression states.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>

    <dl class="facts" aria-label="Cosmic Breath structural facts">
      <div><dt>Selectable TOM states</dt><dd>51</dd></div>
      <div><dt>Expansion states</dt><dd>26</dd></div>
      <div><dt>Compression states</dt><dd>25</dd></div>
      <div><dt>Explicit guarded transitions</dt><dd>2</dd></div>
    </dl>

    <p>
      The explorer is a structural sequence. Its slider is index-based rather than a
      proportional time axis. Selecting a state is educational inspection unless an
      explicit guarded transition is activated.
    </p>

    <div class="anchors" aria-label="Declared CU model-level anchors">
      <article>
        <p class="label">Expansion</p>
        <p class="anchor-value">Approximately 2.8 trillion years</p>
      </article>
      <article>
        <p class="label">Compression</p>
        <p class="anchor-value">Approximately 308 billion years</p>
      </article>
      <article>
        <p class="label">Complete Cosmic Breath</p>
        <p class="anchor-value">Approximately 3.108 trillion years</p>
      </article>
    </div>

    <p class="callout">
      These model-level anchors are declared by the CU framework. They should not be
      reconstructed by silently summing or normalizing the individual TOM duration estimates.
    </p>
  </section>

  <div class="education-grid">
    <section
      class="education-card"
      id="reading-the-structural-diagram"
      aria-labelledby="reading-the-structural-diagram-title"
    >
      <div class="section-heading">
        <span aria-hidden="true">02</span>
        <div>
          <h3 id="reading-the-structural-diagram-title">Reading the Structural Diagram</h3>
          <p>
            The diagram represents structural sequence and relationship, not measured
            physical dimensions.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
      <p>
        Rings, spacing, radial placement, and visible size are not physical measurements
        and are not proportional to elapsed duration. The 51 selectable TOM states form
        the state inventory.
      </p>
      <p>
        Expansion proceeds from <strong>expansion-sub-ztom</strong> through{' '}
        <strong>expansion-atom</strong>. Compression proceeds from
        {' '}<strong>compression-btom</strong> through <strong>compression-ztom</strong>.
        Direct selection lets a visitor inspect any state, but it does not execute either
        guarded transition.
      </p>
      <p class="callout">
        This field is structural and index-based—not drawn to physical or temporal scale.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section
      class="education-card"
      id="tom-names-and-positions"
      aria-labelledby="tom-names-and-positions-title"
    >
      <div class="section-heading">
        <span aria-hidden="true">03</span>
        <div>
          <h3 id="tom-names-and-positions-title">TOM Names and Positions</h3>
          <p>
            TOM names identify positions in the CU structural sequence; similar names do
            not make states interchangeable.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
      <p>
        Expansion names use the <strong>sub-</strong> prefix through sub-btom, followed by
        atom. Compression proceeds from btom through ztom without the expansion prefix.
        Expansion and compression records remain distinct even when their notation or
        duration values look alike.
      </p>
      <dl class="examples">
        <div><dt>expansion-sub-btom</dt><dd>Supercluster formation</dd></div>
        <div><dt>compression-btom</dt><dd>Galactic evolution and contraction</dd></div>
        <div><dt>expansion-sub-ctom</dt><dd>Star life cycle era</dd></div>
        <div><dt>compression-ctom</dt><dd>Final stellar formations</dd></div>
      </dl>
    </section>

    <section
      class="education-card"
      id="exponents-tetration-and-duration"
      aria-labelledby="exponents-tetration-and-duration-title"
    >
      <div class="section-heading">
        <span aria-hidden="true">04</span>
        <div>
          <h3 id="exponents-tetration-and-duration-title">
            Exponents, Tetration, and Duration
          </h3>
          <p>
            Notation identifies mathematical structure; it is not an elapsed-time label.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="notation">
        <article>
          <p class="label">Ordinary exponentiation</p>
          <p class="equation">2¹ = 2 · 2² = 4 · 2⁴ = 16 · 2¹⁶ = 65,536</p>
          <p>A superscript exponent describes repeated multiplication.</p>
        </article>
        <article>
          <p class="label">Tetration</p>
          <p class="equation">2↑↑4</p>
          <p>
            Two upward arrows describe repeated exponentiation: a power tower of 2s whose
            height is the final number.
          </p>
        </article>
      </div>
      <p>
        The notation <strong>2↑↑n</strong> does not mean n seconds. Complete large
        tetration values are neither written out nor calculated here. For example,{' '}
        <strong>2↑↑65,536</strong> is a power tower with height 65,536; that height is
        distinct from ztom’s declared one-second duration.
      </p>
      <p>
        Every TOM retains an exact duration-estimate string. Familiar summaries help
        communicate scale without replacing those strings. Diagram spacing does not encode
        duration, and individual row durations must not be silently added to recreate the
        model-level anchors.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section
      class="education-card"
      id="expansion-compression-and-guarded-boundaries"
      aria-labelledby="expansion-compression-and-guarded-boundaries-title"
    >
      <div class="section-heading">
        <span aria-hidden="true">05</span>
        <div>
          <h3 id="expansion-compression-and-guarded-boundaries-title">
            Expansion, Compression, and Guarded Boundaries
          </h3>
          <p>
            A selectable pause state and the action that crosses its boundary are separate
            parts of the explorer.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="boundary-pairs">
        <article class="boundary-state">
          <p class="label">Selectable state</p>
          <h4>expansion-atom</h4>
          <p>
            The final expansion state, with source concept “Start of compression.” It
            remains in expansion, and ordinary Next cannot cross its boundary.
          </p>
        </article>
        <article class="boundary-action">
          <p class="label">Guarded transition action</p>
          <h4>Begin Compression</h4>
          <p>
            A separate, non-selectable transition into compression-btom with cycle offset{' '}
            <strong>0</strong>. Directly inspecting compression-btom does not execute it.
          </p>
        </article>
        <article class="boundary-state">
          <p class="label">Selectable state</p>
          <h4>compression-ztom</h4>
          <p>
            The final compression state and reset-pause, with source concept “ZTOM: full
            universal reset.” Ordinary Next cannot cross its boundary.
          </p>
        </article>
        <article class="boundary-action">
          <p class="label">Guarded transition action</p>
          <h4>Begin the Next Cosmic Breath</h4>
          <p>
            A separate, non-selectable transition into the next cycle’s expansion-sub-ztom
            with cycle offset <strong>1</strong>. Direct inspection neither executes it nor
            increments the cycle counter.
          </p>
        </article>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section
      class="education-card"
      id="observable-universe-marker"
      aria-labelledby="observable-universe-marker-title"
    >
      <div class="section-heading">
        <span aria-hidden="true">06</span>
        <div>
          <h3 id="observable-universe-marker-title">Observable Universe Marker</h3>
          <p>A visual reference that remains outside the selectable TOM sequence.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
      <p>
        Within the CU diagram, the non-selectable Observable Universe marker is positioned
        structurally between compression-btom and compression-ctom. It is not ctom, not
        sub-ctom, and not one of the 51 selectable TOM states.
      </p>
      <p class="callout">
        This placement is a CU structural proposition — not an empirical measurement. The
        diagram is structural and is not drawn to physical or temporal scale.
      </p>
      <p>
        The marker is not selectable, has no slider position, does not participate in
        direct selection, is not a transition, and does not alter the state inventory.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section
      class="education-card education-card--epistemic"
      id="structural-and-epistemic-boundaries"
      aria-labelledby="structural-and-epistemic-boundaries-title"
    >
      <div class="section-heading">
        <span aria-hidden="true">07</span>
        <div>
          <h3 id="structural-and-epistemic-boundaries-title">
            Structural and Epistemic Boundaries
          </h3>
          <p>Structural organization does not convert a theoretical claim into a measurement.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
      <p class="epistemic-label">
        CU structural proposition — not an empirical measurement
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li>
          CU descriptions are internal claims of the Cosmic Universalism framework, not
          measurements established by contemporary empirical cosmology.
        </li>
        <li>
          Physics-inspired TOM names do not by themselves establish measured physical
          mechanisms.
        </li>
        <li>
          Structural diagrams organize the CU model; they do not replace astronomical
          observations or standard empirical analysis.
        </li>
        <li>
          The page distinguishes source wording from public educational explanation while
          identifying important empirical and interpretive boundaries.
        </li>
      </ul>
    </section>
  </div>

